<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Wabanaki Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1680 CE – 1862 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An alliance of Algonquian peoples of northern New England and the Maritimes, the People of the Dawnland, long allied with New France against the English.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penobscot">Penobscot</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passamaquoddy">Passamaquoddy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliseet">Maliseet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> shared related languages and ties of kinship and trade. In the later 17th century, facing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk</a> raids and English expansion after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a>, they formalised an alliance, often dated to around 1680, with a council fire linked to the Catholic mission villages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each nation kept its own sachems and territories, while the confederacy coordinated diplomacy and war through councils and wampum. Hunting, fishing, gathering and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming supported communities from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merrimack-river">Merrimack</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-cape-breton-island">Cape Breton</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-france">New France</a> and supported by missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastien-rale">Sébastien Rale</a>, the Wabanaki fought the English in a series of wars from 1688, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dummers-war">Father Rale's War</a> in 1722 to 1725. Leaders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penobscot">Penobscot</a> sachem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madockawando">Madockawando</a> dealt with both empires.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-france">New France</a> the nations signed Peace and Friendship Treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. The confederacy gradually lapsed and was formally dissolved in 1862, but it was revived in 1993.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wabanaki-confederacy.jpg" alt="Wabanaki Confederacy" width="320" height="210"><figcaption>Image: Grug-Jack · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wabanaki_Confederacy_(Anglicised_Names).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1680 CE – 1862 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Madockawando</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Algonquian traditional religion, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eastern Algonquian languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wabanaki, Dawnland Confederacy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1771&amp;focus=wabanaki-confederacy" aria-label="Wabanaki Confederacy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2565 153 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wabanaki Confederacy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2885 409h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2885 409h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Canada. <a href="/atlas?year=1771&amp;focus=wabanaki-confederacy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

